today we'll hear from antoine castex  enterprise architect at the cosmetics  company l'oreal  they built a serverless multi-cloud data  warehouse based on google cloud welcome  to the show antoine  thanks martin for having me  did you know that i have been using  google cloud since 2011 starting with  app engine  wow that that's about the same time i  started with app engine uh all right so  why did l'oreal need a new data  warehouse onto one because we have data  in many different databases in many  different systems like any large company  we need to democratize this data and  make it available to thousands of  employees  sounds great i can't wait to hear how  you did it  [Music]  alright first on to one could you tell  me about l'oreal's business and your  data needs yeah sure l'oreal had sales  of 32 billion euros in 2021 we are  present in more than 150 countries we  have many brands adapted to local  requirements and we keep rolling out new  ones wow cosmetics and personal care are  big business i guess  yes they are we need to have a good  understanding of all the data we need  for all our brands and manage  requirements for different countries we  have many brands many organizations  within the company and many retail  stores and of course many systems  so what systems do you use for that our  systems are have grown organically over  the time we collect data from point of  sale systems salesforce sap  microsoft facebook google ads  and so on  you're a large company and any large  companies has a lot of systems and data  sources right  yes that's right but our employee needs  a single view of the data from all of  the system in one place to make business  decision  we need to unify all the different  systems and see data in only one place  that makes sense  how did you manage the complexity of  that  well we decided early on that the data  warehouse will be no ops it must be  sustainable and we will use e l d  instead of the traditional etl  okay let's talk about each one of those  for a minute so first off you said no  ops what do you mean by no ops  oh i mean the job of a developer at  loyal is not to manage servers we need  infrastructure that scale on demand so  that our developer can deliver business  value instead of managing server that  makes sense how did you accomplish that  we decided to put the data in bigquery  with scales up and down automatically  i'm sure google is using servers to do  that but we don't have to deal with them  to load and transform the data we pick  it up cloud function and cloudon which  are also servers  got it  next you said your data warehouse needs  to be environmentally sustainable  yes of course this is very very  important to us and to our customer we  need to know what the carbon footprint  is for our data warehouse and we were  happy to see that google clouds include  a cable footprint dashboard because it  helped tell us about the footprint but  also how to reduce it  great i love that carbon footprint  dashboard as well i use that for all my  side projects so the third point you  mentioned is elt now i know of etl and  that means extract transform load if i  remember correctly and it's a way to  pull data from systems and to make it  useful for decision makers but what is e  lt  you're right martin etl is the  traditional approach and i used that in  my previous job and it was working well  but now  more than data warehouse like bigquery  can transform loaded data so eld is  faster and cheaper than etl  oh uh how much faster and cheaper  well we used to spend thousands of euro  on data transformation that took hour  by doing elt with bigquery we spend tens  of euro and it takes only minutes  wow from thousands of euros to tens of  euros i like that i might have to look  into elt for for next system i'm  building  okay so what was the architecture of  this data warehouse  the job of a data warehouse is to  connect data sources with data consumer  okay and before we continue what's an  example of a data consumer at loyal  that's a great question it could be a  marketing team who needs to know how  many bottles of ifs and perfume were  sold in europe versus in asia versus in  latin america  i see and how many data consumers like  that are there within l'oreal  right now we have onboarded about five  thousand users but the goal at the end  is to have thirty thousand data consumer  which represents thirty percent of the  company we need to democratize this data  so everyone can make smarter  decisions wow that's a lot of people 30  000 data consumers all right so sorry  for the interruption about data  consumers uh please continue but the  architecture and one yeah no problem the  consumer needs the data presented in a  visual way we will dashboard in looker  for that  looker okay got it and looker pulls the  data from bigquery and we like bigquery  because it's a serverless database that  means no ops like i mentioned before and  also it means that we can adopt a sql  standard as a universal data language or  analyst already nosql  very nice and now using bigquery how  does that data get into bigquery  there is two way  either the data source called an api  based on rpg  or they upload data file directly to  google cloud storage  makes sense  once we have received the data with an  api call or an uploaded file we use  cladren to do some basic cleaning and  formatting operation  okay so is that where the data  transformations happen like swapping out  country names for country codes and so  on  no no no it's not we are using elt so  the data first need to be loaded into  bigquery as soon as possible  and when that is done we can start  transforming it  oh right you're using elt not etl  you load the data first and then you  transform it  that's right now some data set requires  up to  10 transformation  it's important do steps happen in the  right order so to do that we have  defined processes in cloud workflows  oh okay uh how does that work  it is pretty simple we write up the  process steps in a eml file each steps  is a call to bigquery api with some sql  query attached to it  oh so you don't have to write code in  java or some other programming language  to do those transformations  right  it's all declarative with uml files and  sql  that means less work for our data  engineer to create the transformations  i like that less code means fewer bugs  uh now how are these cloud workflows  triggered  the workflows are triggered by an event  arc event every time a cloudron service  runs an event is generated automatically  by google we don't have to create an  event ourself we just need to consume  events  it is like magic  i love it when it's like magic  how many of these workflows do you have  we have  about  eight thousand and five hundred  different flows of data in the data  warehouse and there is one workflow for  each one  wow that's a lot of workflows i can see  why you don't want to write a separate  java or python program for each one of  those  yes sure that would probably not scale  right  okay so it sounds like a big data  warehouse but one that's pretty easy to  run  what plans do you have next next is more  sequel than ever we want to replace most  of our imperative python code with  declarative sql  sql again is the universal language  within the company so the less we do in  other language the easier things we will  be false  sounds exciting thank you for joining me  today antoine and thank you everyone for  watching if you have questions for  antoine or me please write a comment  below also let me know what other  serverless topics you'd like to see on  the show  see you next time  [Music]

